-- title:
--   Degree-one eta-sections on the ξ-branch of the edge family
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota\colon W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to W(k)$ (the source `Zp2 p` being the Witt vectors of the field with $p^2$ elements). Write $\bar W := W(k)/(p)$ for the quotient by the ideal generated by $p$, and let $\bar\jmath := \mathrm{mk}\circ\iota$ be the induced map $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to \bar W$. Let $E :=$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar W$ be the edge chart ring over $\bar W$ (the ring `FormalOmega.chartERing` of $\bar W$ at $0$), with its two distinguished elements $\xi, \eta$, and let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra.
--
--   The data on the fixed module: $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $\bar W$ (a commutative two-dimensional formal group law with an action of `Zp2 p` and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying the usual compatibilities), subject to: `hΦ`, which says that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. the weight-$0$ and weight-$1$ parts of its Lie algebra are complementary and both invertible; `hΦ4`, which says that multiplication by $p$ on $\Phi$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, which says that the weight-$0$ part of the Lie algebra is contained in the kernel of the linear part of $\varpi$; and `hcΦ`, which says that the graded pieces of degree $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ (the subgroups on which the Teichmüller action of $\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ is through $\bar\jmath(\,\cdot\,)^{p^n}$, $n = 0, 1$) are complementary. Attached to these is an additive map $r_\Phi\colon (\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathbb{Z}_p) \to$ `NMod` of the graded Cartier module data of $\Phi$, and the rigidification hypothesis `hrΦ`: for every additive $L$ from the Cartier module of $\Phi$ to its `NMod` which is a canonical $L$-map, $r_\Phi$ maps the whole of its source bijectively onto the degree-$0$ eta-piece `etaPiece L _ 0` cut out by $L$.
--
--   The matrix: $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ with `hg` stipulating that $g$ is the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(p, 1)$.
--
--   The edge family: $X$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $E$ and $\gamma\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to$ `CartierModule p X.F` is a pair of Cartier-module elements, subject to: `hγ`, that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $\iota$ followed by $\bar W \to E$ (each $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of degree $i$, and the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_j)$ has unit determinant); `hγa`, that $X$ has structure constants the edge constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p` $\bar W$ formed from $\xi$ and $\eta$, i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi(\gamma_i)$ agrees with $\sum_{m<N} V^{m}([a_{m,i}]\gamma_{\pi(m,i)})$ modulo $V^{N}$; `hXs`, that $X$ is special for the same structure map; and `hX4`, that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   The node specialisation: $f_0\colon E \to \bar W$ is a ring homomorphism with `hf₀`, that $f_0$ composed after the algebra map $\bar W \to E$ is the identity on $\bar W$, and `hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η`, that $f_0(\xi) = f_0(\eta) = 0$.
--
--   The isogeny at the node and its rigidification: $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\rho_0$ a pair of power series over $\bar W$ with `hρ₀`, that $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi \to X \otimes_{f_0} \bar W$ of height $4m$ (a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$). Writing $t_0$ for the rigidified datum `Rigidified.mk` with module $X \otimes_{f_0} \bar W$, level $m$ and series the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo the ideal generated by $p$, the hypotheses are: `hOD₀`, that the series of $t_0$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar X$ of $t_0$; `hcb`, that the graded pieces of degree $0$ and $1$ of the reduction of $t_0$ along the identity of $\bar W$ are complementary; `hcΦg`, the corresponding complementarity for $\Phi$ along the identity. Finally $a \in \mathbb{N}$ and the normalisation `hN`: for both $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$, $p^a$ times the value of `Rigidified.rigidNum` (built from $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$, $t_0$, `hOD₀`, the identity of $\bar W$, `hcb` and `hcΦg`) at the $i$-th standard vector equals $p^{a+m}$ times the $i$-th entry of the pair whose $0$-th entry is `nMk` of $(\gamma_0$ base-changed along $f_0$, then modulo $p$, then along the reduction of the identity$, 0)$ and whose $1$-st entry is `nVarpi` applied to `nMk` of the same base change of $\gamma_1$ paired with $0$.
--
--   The edge isogeny: $\rho_1$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules from $(X \otimes_{f_0} \bar W) \otimes_{\bar W} E$ to $X$, subject to the explicit formulae `hρ₁0`, `hρ₁1` for its action on Cartier modules,
--   $$\rho_1(\gamma_0) = p\,\gamma_0 - V([\eta^{p^2}]\,\gamma_1), \qquad \rho_1(\gamma_1) = p\,\gamma_1 - V([\xi^{p^2}]\,\gamma_0),$$
--   where the $\gamma_i$ on the left are base-changed along $f_0$ and then along $\bar W \to E$, $[\,\cdot\,]$ is the Teichmüller lift and $V$ is the integral Verschiebung; and `hρ₁h`, that $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   The point: $y\colon E \to \Omega$ is a ring homomorphism with `hyξ`, that $y(\xi) \neq 0$.
--
--   Let $t$ denote the rigidified datum over $\Omega$ obtained by pushing `Rigidified.mk` with module $X$, level $m+1$ and series the composite of ($\rho_1$ reduced modulo the ideal generated by $p$ in $E$) after ($\rho_0$ mapped along $\bar W \to E$ and then reduced) along $y$; let $\psi$ be the structure homomorphism $y$ composed after $\bar W \to E$ and after $W(k) \to \bar W$, and let $s :=$ `Rigidified.awayHom (1 : Ω)` be the localisation of $\Omega$ away from $1$. Then the following exist:
--
--   (i) a proof `hOD` that the series of $t$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules from $\bar\Phi$ of $t$ to $\bar X$ of $t$ over the reduction of $\Omega$;
--
--   (ii) proofs `hc`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces are complementary, respectively for $X$ of $t$ base-changed along $s$, for its reduction, and for $\Phi$ base-changed along $s$;
--
--   (iii) an additive map $L$ from the Cartier module $D.M$ of $X$ of $t$ along $s$ to $D.\mathrm{NMod}$, where $D$ is the graded Cartier module data determined by `hc`, together with a proof `hL` that $L$ is a canonical $L$-map (a Cartier $L$-map admitting a lift over a $p$-torsion-free surjective cover carrying special Cartier module data);
--
--   (iv) two elements $z_0, z_1 \in D.\mathrm{NMod}$ which are eta-sections of index $1$ with coordinates $p^{-1}e_0$ and $p^{-1}e_1$: hypotheses `hz₀`, `hz₁` assert, for $i = 0, 1$ respectively, that $z_i$ lies in the degree-$1$ eta-piece `etaPiece L _ 1` and that the relation `LatticeRel` holds, at level $m+1$ and for the rigid numeration `rigidNum` of $t$, between the reduction `etaRed` of `nVarpi` applied once to $z_i$ and $p \cdot (p^{-1} e_i)$, where $e_i$ is the $i$-th standard basis vector of the standard full lattice in $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$;
--
--   (v) elements $m_0, m_1 \in D.M$ such that: the class of $m_0$ modulo the image `vRange` of the Verschiebung equals the value of the map $D.u$ (attached to $L$ and the Verschiebung compatibility of its Cartier $L$-map) at $z_0$ regarded as an element of the eta-subgroup; likewise the class of $m_1$ equals $D.u$ at $z_1$; for every $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$ one has $\mathrm{tangent}(m_1)_j = -\,s(y(\xi))\cdot \mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j$; and $\mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j \neq 0$ for at least one $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$.
--
--   This is the $\xi$-branch computation in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: at a point $y$ of the edge chart with $y(\xi) \neq 0$ it produces, for the explicit edge family rigidified by the composite of the node isogeny of height $4m$ with the edge isogeny of height $4$, a canonical $L$-map and a pair of degree-one eta-sections whose rigidified coordinates are $p^{-1}e_0$ and $p^{-1}e_1$ and whose tangent vectors are proportional with ratio $-y(\xi)$. It is used in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_edge_of_apply_xi_ne_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_edge_of_apply_xi_ne_zero) to identify the kernel of the associated isogeny as the span determined by the lattice datum.
